<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

    <worksheet margin="1in" bottom="1.25cm" xml:id="vectors-complete">
    <title>Vectors (Complete)</title>
        <page>
        
            <exercise workspace="2in">
            <title>Motivation</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        Vectors are useful tools when you need to specify both magnitude and direction. We'll develop 
                        some basics of working with vectors.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </exercise>

            <exercise workspace="6in">
            <title>First Things</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        Scalars specify magnitude only, while vectors specify both magnitude and direction. Vectors are 
                        uniquely determined by their magnitude and direction, not their position in space!
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Sometimes we do want to place a vector somewhere particular in space. A <term>position vector</term> is a vector drawn from the origin.
                    </p>
                 
                </solution>
            </exercise>
</page>
<page>
            <exercise workspace="3in">
                <title>Computations with Vectors</title>
             
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                        Let <m>\vvec_1 = \langle 2,3 \rangle</m> and <m>\vvec_2 = \langle -2,4 \rangle</m>.
            
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
                            Computing magnitude in direction is the same process used in converting complex numbers to 
                            polar coordinates.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            Reviewing what was done there, make sure you can compute that 
                            <md>
                                |\vvec_1| = \sqrt{13}, |\vvec_2| = \sqrt{20}
                            </md>
                            and the angles of <m>\vvec_1</m> and <m>\vvec_2</m> are <m>\arctan(3/2)</m> and <m>\arctan(-2) + \pi</m>, respectively.
                            
                        </p>
                    </solution>
          
            </exercise>
</page>
<page>
        <exercise workspace="8in">
        <title>Combining Vectors</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
              Let <m>\vvec_1 = \langle 2,3 \rangle</m> and <m>\vvec_2 = \langle -2,4 \rangle</m>
            </p>
           
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Addition.
                            <md>
                                <mrow>\vvec_1 + \vvec_2 \amp= \langle 2 +(-2), 3+4 \rangle </mrow>
                                <mrow> \amp= \langle 0,7 \rangle </mrow>
                            </md>
                            Note addition is commutative. 
                            
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Subtraction. 
                            <md>
                                <mrow>\vvec_1 - \vvec_2 \amp= \langle 4,-1 \rangle </mrow>
                            </md>
                            and 
                            <md>
                                <mrow>\vvec_2 - \vvec_1 \amp= \langle -4,1 \rangle </mrow>
                            </md>
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            These are different vectors, and can be visualized as <term>displacement vectors</term>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Scalar Multiplication. 
                            <md>
                                <mrow>2 \cdot \vvec_1 \amp= \langle 4, 6\rangle </mrow>
                                <mrow> -0.5 \cdot \vvec_2 \amp = \langle 1, -2 \rangle </mrow>
                            </md>
                            We'll discuss other notions of multiplication later this week.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
            <p>
                These operations can also be visualized, which we'll practice more in lab.
            </p>
        </solution>
            
        </exercise>
</page>
<page>

        <exercise workspace="5in">
        <title>Unit Vectors</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                <term>Unit vectors</term> are vectors <m>\uvec</m> such that
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <m>|\uvec| = 1</m>. 
            </p>
            <p>
                For example, <m>\mathbf{i}, \mathbf{j}, \mathbf{j}</m> are the unit vectors <m>\langle 1, 0, 0 \rangle, \langle 0, 1, 0 \rangle, \langle 0, 0, 1 \rangle</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                We can also <term>normalize</term> a non-zero vector <m>\vvec</m> by scalar multiplying the vector by <m>\frac{1}{|\vvec|}</m>. 
            </p>
        </solution>
            
        </exercise>
    
    </page>


       
    </worksheet>
